## Quick start

```bash
cp .env.example .env
uv sync
uv run {{ package_slug }}
```

The default `.env.example` points at the Postgres and Redis services defined in
`docker-compose.yml`. Start them first if you don't have your own instances running:

```bash
docker compose up -d db redis
uv run alembic upgrade head
uv run {{ package_slug }}
```

`uv run {{ package_slug }}` is the canonical way to run the app locally; it
reads `host`/`port` from `Settings` (`.env`). Because the project depends on
`fastapi[standard]`, `uv run fastapi dev` and `uv run fastapi run` also work
(reading the `[tool.fastapi]` entrypoint in `pyproject.toml` instead of
`Settings`) if you want `fastapi dev`'s autoreload during development —
just note its `--host`/`--port` flags are independent of `{{ env_prefix }}HOST`/
`{{ env_prefix }}PORT`.

## Running with Docker

```bash
cp .env.example .env
docker compose up --build
```

This builds the application image and starts it alongside Postgres and Redis. The API
is available at `http://localhost:8000`.

## Quality checks

```bash
uv run ruff check .
uv run ruff format .
uv run ty check
uv run pytest
uv run library-skills --tool-skill install --claude --yes --all
uv tool install prek
prek install -t pre-commit -t commit-msg -t pre-push
prek run --all-files
```

`prek install` with no `-t` flags only wires up the `pre-commit` stage; the
conventional-commit check (`commit-msg`) and the pytest gate (`pre-push`)
configured in `prek.toml` need their hook types installed explicitly.

## Migrations

```bash
uv run alembic upgrade head
uv run alembic revision --autogenerate -m "add new model"
```

## Testing

Tests run against a real Postgres database and a real Redis instance rather
than mocks, so both need to be reachable before running `uv run pytest`:

```bash
docker compose up -d db redis
```

By default tests use:

- `{{ env_prefix }}TEST_DATABASE_URL` (defaults to
  `postgresql+asyncpg://{{ package_name }}:{{ package_name }}@localhost:5432/{{ package_name }}_test`)
- `{{ env_prefix }}TEST_REDIS_URL` (defaults to `redis://localhost:6379/1`)

Override either if your local Postgres/Redis use different credentials or
ports. A session-scoped fixture in `tests/conftest.py` applies Alembic
migrations to the test database automatically before the suite runs. CI
provisions matching Postgres and Redis service containers (see
`.github/workflows/ci.yml`).

## Queue backends

Use environment variables to switch backends:

- `{{ env_prefix }}QUEUE_BACKEND=memory`
- `{{ env_prefix }}QUEUE_BACKEND=redis`
- `{{ env_prefix }}QUEUE_BACKEND=azure_storage_queue`

Define scopes with JSON in `{{ env_prefix }}SCOPES`, for example:

```json
[
  {"name": "identity", "description": "Authentication and authorization."},
  {"name": "orders", "description": "Ordering workflows."}
]
```

## API conventions

- `/health` reports queue reachability and a real database liveness check
  (a pooled connection checkout through the async engine), not just URL-shape
  validation.
{% if include_example_endpoint -%}
- `/items` is a real, Postgres-backed example endpoint demonstrating paginated
  collections (`page_size=20` by default, `100` as the maximum) with a
  request-scoped `AsyncSession` dependency and a repository. Replace it with
  your own domain endpoints.
{% endif -%}
- `/tasks/dispatch` queues long-running work to the configured queue backend.

Routes are grouped into per-domain `APIRouter`s in `routes/router.py`
(`system_router`{% if include_example_endpoint %}, `items_router`{% endif %}, `tasks_router`), each declaring its own
`prefix`/`tags` rather than repeating them per route — follow the same
pattern when adding new domains.

## Correlation IDs and request tracing

All requests automatically receive a correlation ID for backend tracing:

- Correlation IDs are generated internally for every request (UUID v4).
- Correlation IDs are included in all response headers as `x-correlation-id`.
- Correlation IDs are propagated through logs in the format:
  ```
  [correlation_id=<id> trace_id=<trace> span_id=<span>]
  ```

Use the response header value to trace a request through backend logs:

```bash
curl -i http://localhost:8000/health
```

**Backend observability**: Correlation IDs are intended for server-side request
inspection. Clients do not supply them; downstream services should use the internally
generated value when propagating backend trace context.

## Agent skill discovery

Run `uv run library-skills --tool-skill install --claude --yes --all` after
dependency changes to refresh `.agents/skills` and `.claude/skills` symlinks
for libraries that ship agent skills.
